---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { loadComponents } from '../lib/components';
import { GITHUB_URL, STORYBOOK_URL } from '../lib/repo';
import { PAGE_SOURCES, freshness } from '../lib/sources';
import { loadTokens } from '../lib/tokens';

const { primitives, semantic } = loadTokens();
const components = loadComponents();

const principles = [
  { title: 'The repo decides', text: 'Tokens and components in code are the source of truth. Figma explores ideas and receives what shipped.' },
  { title: 'Meaning before value', text: 'Designs use semantic tokens (action, feedback, surface), never raw colors. Themes swap values, not decisions.' },
  { title: 'Accessible by default', text: 'Native elements, visible keyboard focus, labels wired to fields, and color is never the only signal.' },
  { title: 'Guardrails over good intentions', text: 'Rules that matter are checked by CI on every change, for people and AI agents alike.' },
  { title: 'Written once, shown everywhere', text: 'These pages, Storybook and the agent context are generated from the same files. Nothing is typed twice.' },
];
---

<Layout title="Home" description="What this design system is, how it works and how to contribute." sources={PAGE_SOURCES.home} updated={freshness(PAGE_SOURCES.home)}>
  <h1>Design system documentation</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A small design system run as code: {primitives.length} primitive tokens, {semantic.length} semantic tokens in light and dark,
    and {components.length} components. Every page here is generated from the repository at build time, so what you read is what ships.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Demo repository.</strong> It illustrates an operating model (design system as code, evolved with an AI agent through reviewed
      pull requests), not a production library. One brand, two themes, four components.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {
      principles.map((p, i) => (
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>
            {i + 1}. {p.title}
          </h3>
          <p>{p.text}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Start here</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <a class="panel" href="/foundations/color/"><h3>Foundations</h3><p>Color in both themes, contrast checks, type, spacing, radius and elevation.</p></a>
    <a class="panel" href="/components/"><h3>Components</h3><p>When to use each one, do and don't, accessibility and the tokens it reads.</p></a>
    <a class="panel" href="/governance/"><h3>Governance</h3><p>Who approves what, how a token change travels, and what the AI agent may do.</p></a>
    <a class="panel" href="/changelog/"><h3>Changelog</h3><p>What changed, what it means for you, and a message ready to post.</p></a>
  </div>

  <h2>How to contribute</h2>
  <p>Every change, from anyone, lands as a pull request that people review. Pick the path that fits you:</p>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>Designers, from Figma</h3>
      <p>
        Explore in Figma, then send the proposal with the <strong>DS Bridge</strong> plugin. It opens a pull request with the token or component
        change, so you never edit code by hand. CI checks it and a design owner and a tech owner review it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>Anyone, with an issue</h3>
      <p>
        Not sure what the change should be? <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues/new`}>Open an issue</a> with the problem, a screenshot or a Figma
        link. A maintainer or the AI agent turns it into a pull request.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>The AI agent, by pull request</h3>
      <p>
        The agent works on its own branch, follows <a href="/for-ai-agents/">the same rules shown here</a>, and opens a pull request. It never merges:
        people review and decide, with the same risk tiers as everyone else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={STORYBOOK_URL}>Storybook</a>: every component state, in light and dark, with the accessibility panel.</li>
    <li><a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub repository</a>: token sources, components and pull requests.</li>
    <li><a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>: this documentation as plain text for AI agents.</li>
  </ul>
</Layout>
